; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; AUTHORED -- there is no upstream .bddl for this task.
;
; provenance.tier: designed
; reference: channel routing.mp4, a bimanual cable-routing demonstration
;
; WHAT THE PROBLEM IS. Every other cable variant in this tree routes a rod
; AGAINST fixtures it lies visibly on top of -- a peg it winds onto, a line of
; spools it weaves between. This one puts the rod INSIDE a channel for most of
; the episode, and what makes it hard is not aim: a pushed rod is a column, a
; column buckles, and a buckled rod inside a tube stops being a rod being fed
; and becomes a rod wedged against two walls.
;
; THE CHANNEL IS ON A BOX, AND THE BOX IS FREE. The reference clip's channel is
; the lidded front edge of a plastic cable-management box standing on the mat,
; and a person feeding a cable into it holds the box still with the other
; hand. So the channel is an OBJECT here rather than a fixture: a 400 x 200 x
; 100 mm box the cable is fed through, mirrored into the cable's solver two
; ways, that the support hand pinches by its lid strip for the whole feed.
;
; THE CABLE IS TERMINATED, AND THE CONNECTOR IS WHAT THE BORE IS SIZED TO. A
; 15 mm moulded plug in a 17 mm bore has 1 mm of clearance a side, against the
; 10 mm cable's 3.5 -- so the tight dimension is the head, which is where a real
; routing job has it. The head is rigid, wider than what trails it, and cannot
; roll broadside (its diagonal is 18 mm), so it can tilt and snag but not turn
; over. See tunnel/plug.py and tunnel/box.py::BORE.
;
; SUCCESS IS THAT THE FAR TIP CAME OUT AND WAS DRAWN CLEAR. Not that the cable
; is near the box, not that it entered -- it started entered. The tip, past the
; lid's far end and along the open ledge beyond it, with the rod still lying in
; the bore behind it.
;
; THE SECOND CLAUSE IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS ISN'T ONE
; PREDICATE. `end_b` inside a box beyond the exit is satisfied perfectly well by
; a policy that lets go, picks the cable up, and carries the tip around the
; OUTSIDE of the box. A 250 mm channel has an outside, so the goal has to say
; that the route went through it.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem THREAD_THE_CABLE_THROUGH_THE_CHANNEL)


  (:language feed the cable into the cable box's channel and draw the plug out along the far ledge)

  ; The cell, the control mode, the episode count and (:arms 2) are
  ; rgbs.suite's; so is the pairing, and it is worth reading here anyway.
  (:staging
    ; THE RIGHT HAND FEEDS AND THE LEFT HAND HOLDS THE BOX, which is what the
    ; reference clip shows and is not a cosmetic hold any more: the box is a
    ; free body and nothing but the pinch keeps it where the regions were
    ; measured. Two hands on the cable is the obvious reading of "bimanual"
    ; and it does not fit: the only place a hand can take the rod is the 90 mm
    ; between the flare and the free span that buckles behind it, and two
    ; grippers do not share 90 mm.
    ;
    ; WHICH ARM DOES WHICH IS MEASURED. The two hands are mirror images, so the
    ; same grasp orientation puts one across the cable and the other along it --
    ; measured at the grasp, the right hand's jaws sit 69 mm apart across the
    ; rod and 4 mm along it, and the left hand's sit 4 mm across and 56 mm
    ; along. The hand that can actually hold a rod is the right one, so the
    ; right one feeds. See routine_tunnel.py. That pairing is the suite's own
    ; -- right works, left holds, arms solid -- so it is inherited.
    (:physics cable_tunnel_960hz@v1)
    ; The suite rig, aimed at the rod's axis mid-run, 97 mm up: the centre of
    ; the lidded channel rather than of the work, because that is what puts the
    ; hands' 90 mm outside the entry and the goal 300 mm the other way in one
    ; frame.
    (:cameras tunnel.layout:THREAD_VIEWS)
    ; The routine's whole choreography: a settle, the support hand's descent
    ; onto the lid, feed strokes of approach-close-push-release-lift until the
    ; tip is out (five to nine of them -- a stroke nets about three quarters of
    ; its push), the release, and the plug draw. A stroke costs up to 780 steps,
    ; most of which is the release WAITING for the jaws to actually open: a pad
    ; still on the cable drags it, so the wait is a condition rather than a
    ; count (routine_tunnel.py::OPEN_WAIT). Six strokes and the draw is about
    ; 5900. The ceiling is set above what the script needs rather than against
    ; it, because what a POLICY needs is not what a script needs.
    (:max-steps 8000)
  )

  ; Placed by their records: the box where the reach solve and the rod's tail
  ; put it, and the rod laid straight through its mouth, level with the bore,
  ; both tunnel/layout.py's.
  (:objects
    cable - tunnel_cable
    ; A FREE BODY, and it was a fixture. The record declares domain rigid and
    ; contacts with the cable; the composer mirrors it into the cable's island
    ; two ways, so the feed can shove an unheld box and the support hand's
    ; pinch is what stops it. Its bore is CLOSED -- walls, a lid, a floor --
    ; rather than the open slot the clip's box has: an open channel is filled
    ; from above, which makes the task a press, and a bore has only one way
    ; through it.
    box - cable_box
  )

  ; No fixtures. The table is the cell's, and everything else is the box.

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; THREE REGIONS, all boxes in the BOX's own frame, whose origin sits on the
  ; bore axis at the centre of the lidded run, at the bore floor's level --
  ; 100.5 mm above the table, which is the box's top: the mat, the shelf the
  ; channel stands on and the ledge past it are one flat surface.
  ;
  ; A REGION CARRIES NO ROTATION. RegionHandle offsets a world-axis-aligned box
  ; by its target's ORIGIN and applies nothing else -- the origin is re-read
  ; from the live body, so a box that has been shoved carries its regions with
  ; it, but a box that has been TURNED does not carry them round. Which is why
  ; the box stands square to the bench with its run along +y, and why the
  ; support hand holds it.
  ;
  ; These numbers are tunnel/box.py's BORE, RUN, BOX_LENGTH and
  ; layout.py's EXIT_CLEAR written out by hand, because BDDL has no arithmetic.
  ; tests/test_rgbs_tunnel.py reads both and fails when they disagree -- which
  ; is the only thing standing between this file and the silent drift that a
  ; retyped number always eventually is.
  (:regions
    ; The bore itself: 17 mm square, 250 mm long, floor to lid. Sized to the
    ; plug (15 mm across) rather than to the 10 mm cable -- see box.py::BORE.
    ; A capsule's CENTRE is what containment counts, and inside the channel a
    ; 10 mm cable's centre can only be 3.5 mm off the axis, so the width here
    ; is generous by construction and the y bound is what does the work.
    (inside_bore
      (:target box)
      (:ranges ((-0.0085 -0.125 0.0085 0.125)))
      (:z-range 0.0 0.017)
    )
    ; The exit half of the same bore, and it exists for the REWARD rather than
    ; for the goal -- see below. Empty at t = 0 by construction: the seeded tip
    ; sits 45 mm short of this box's near face, and the settle pulls it further
    ; back, not forward.
    (far_half
      (:target box)
      (:ranges ((-0.0085 0.000 0.0085 0.125)))
      (:z-range 0.0 0.017)
    )
    ; Along the open ledge past the lid's far end, and far enough past it that
    ; THE FEED ALONE DOES NOT REACH IT. The feed stops once `end_b` is 20 mm
    ; past the exit, and one more full stroke from just under that reaches 75;
    ; this box begins 80 mm past it, at +0.205, and what
    ; closes the gap is the second hand letting go of the box, taking the plug
    ; where it has emerged onto the ledge, and drawing it 70 mm clear -- so the
    ; goal is a state that move is needed to reach rather than one the feed
    ; happens to pass through. See layout.py::EXIT_CLEAR for the two clearances
    ; that were wrong before this one. The far edge, +0.285, is 10 mm past the
    ; box's own left end (+0.275); a head drawn off the ledge falls 100 mm and
    ; leaves the z window. The z window is stated for the same reason both of
    ; the others are: the default runs to 0.15, which is 127 mm above the lid,
    ; and would count a cable carried over the top of the box.
    (beyond_exit
      (:target box)
      (:ranges ((-0.020 0.205 0.020 0.285)))
      (:z-range 0.0 0.030)
    )
  )

  ; NO INIT CONSTRAINT. Both arms start at the embodiment's own home, touching
  ; nothing -- the convention the weaves were deliberately moved to, and what
  ; makes two runs comparable by eye.
  ;
  ; The tip's 80 mm inside the mouth is a SPAWN POSITION, not a held pose and not
  ; a robot posture: the cable is laid in the shape the clip's own cable has --
  ; flat on the mat behind the box, up the 130 mm hang this rod actually holds,
  ; then along the bore axis and 80 mm past the entry face (assets/tunnel/
  ; layout.py::cable_nodes). Laid straight along the axis instead, the 360 mm
  ; behind the entry hangs in mid-air, falls, and levers the tip 33 mm back out
  ; of the bore before a hand has moved. What that buys is that the episode
  ; is the feed rather than the aim, which is the half of the problem worth
  ; simulating -- a floppy tip found by a gripper is a task about perception,
  ; and this one is a task about what a cable does once it is in.
  (:init)

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; TWO GROUPS UNDER worst_of, and the grouping is the reward design.
  ;
  ; A DO-NOTHING POLICY SCORES ZERO. At t = 0 the tip is 80 mm in, so
  ; `containment cable inside_bore` already reads about 0.18 -- seven capsules
  ; of thirty-eight -- but `end_b` is nowhere near `beyond_exit`, so
  ; the route group is 0/2 while release-and-rest is 2/2, and the min is 0. A
  ; flat conjunction of these four clauses would have paid 0.5 for standing
  ; still.
  ;
  ; THE 0.30 IS ARITHMETIC. When the tip has just cleared the exit, exactly the
  ; run's 250 mm of a 440 mm rod is inside the bore -- 0.57. A rod that buckled
  ; and folded on itself presents less; one that was carried around the outside
  ; presents nearly none. 0.30 sits below the first and far above the second,
  ; and well above the 0.18 the scene starts at.
  ;
  ; NOTHING LATCHES. A cable that pokes out, is let go of, and springs back into
  ; the channel is scored where it ends up.
  (:goal
    (worst_of
      (And
        (>= (containment cable.end_b beyond_exit) 1.0)
        (>= (containment cable inside_bore) 0.30)
      )
      (And
        ; The FEEDING hand let go. Against the nearest segment, because release
        ; is a claim about the whole rod -- a hand off the middle but closed on
        ; one end has not let go.
        ;
        ; IT IS THE WORK ARM ONLY, AND THAT IS WORTH STATING BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS
        ; READING IS WRONG. `tcp_distance` resolves one arm -- `handles.work_arm`
        ; -- and measures from its tool centre (examples/10_rope/rod/measures.py). In a
        ; bimanual layout the support hand can be closed on the cable while this
        ; reads clear: measured on the conduit at 185 mm with the left hand
        ; gripping the connector. So this clause says "the hand that was feeding
        ; has come off", which is the thing worth asserting about a feed, and it
        ; does not say the cable is untouched. The routine's own ordering
        ; depends on knowing the difference -- see PULL_SEAT in routine_tunnel.py.
        (> (tcp-distance cable) 0.05)
        ; Still moving is still being fed. The mean of the segment SPEEDS, not
        ; the speed of the mean.
        (<= (speed cable) 0.05)
      )
    )
  )

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; HOW FAR IT GOT, not what share of the goal is true.
  ;
  ; `fraction` is wrong here in the way adding_a_task.md warns about: with a
  ; two-clause route group, a run that fed 230 of the 250 mm and stopped scores
  ; identically to one that never moved. What is wanted is the front's progress
  ; along the run, and that is what a share of the rod inside the FAR HALF is:
  ; 0 until the tip passes the midpoint, then rising as the cable fills it, then
  ; flat at 0.28 -- 125 mm of a 440 mm rod -- from the moment the tip reaches
  ; the exit face onward.
  ;
  ; MEASURED OVER THE FAR HALF AND NOT THE WHOLE BORE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE
  ; IDLE FLOOR. The seeded tip already puts seven capsules inside
  ; `inside_bore`, so a reward read there would pay a do-nothing policy against
  ; a 0.30 gate -- about 0.6 for standing still, which is exactly the failure
  ; adding_a_task.md says to run the idle floor to catch. Every other progress
  ; reward in this corpus reads zero at t = 0 and so does this one.
  ;
  ; The 0.27 gate is a hair under the 0.284 a full far half reads, so the reward
  ; saturates as the tip arrives at the exit rather than a stroke after it.
  (:reward (progress (containment cable far_half) (:gate 0.27)))

  ; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Recorded every step, gating nothing.
  ;
  ; `straightness` is the one to read beside the reward. This rod starts
  ; straight and then DRAPES -- its tail falls off the box's end to the table
  ; -- so it runs well under 1.0 from the settle on, and what to read is the
  ; trend: a run whose straightness falls while the reward stalls has folded
  ; outside the mouth; one that stalls with it steady is jammed on friction.
  ; Those are different failures and the reward alone cannot tell them apart.
  (:observe
    (once (>= (containment cable.end_b beyond_exit) 1.0))
    (containment cable inside_bore)
    (containment cable.midpoint inside_bore)
    (straightness cable)
    (speed cable)
    (speed box)
  )


  (:policy-params
    ; Main ships no routine runner: routine.py:SolutionRoutine reads this and runs it.
    (routine "routine_tunnel:RoutineTunnel")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier designed)
    (:source "authored beside cable_route, after the bimanual cable-routing clip channel routing.mp4")
    (:note "A 0.44 m 38-capsule rod at 10 mm gauge with a 15 mm moulded plug, fed through the
            250 mm lidded channel of a free 400 x 200 x 100 mm cable box with a 17 mm square
            bore and a flared mouth. Gauge and capsule pitch are cable_route's weave rod, reused
            so the bend stiffness derives at material parity by the same arithmetic against the
            same reference. The box is sized off the clip's mat grid and power strip; the run and
            the rod's length are set together by the bench (the tail has to land on the table)
            and the route (the rod has to cover the run, the exit clearance and a tail). The
            80 mm seed, the 0.30 share and the 80 mm exit region are all pinned to the geometry
            above rather than fitted to a run; the seed's 33 mm of settle slip is measured.")
  )

)
